  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nDry conditions will continue the remainder of the day today. Showers\nand isolated thunderstorms will move into the region overnight and\nthrough the morning daytime hours.  \n\nThe more concerning time period for severe weather and flooding\npotential will be during the afternoon and evening hours on Saturday\nas a low pressure system nears the area. There is decent instability\nand low level shear. LCLs are also expected to be low. While the\nthreat is conditional, there is the potential for a few short lived\ntornadoes to occur in this environment. The greatest threat area for\ntornadoes will be eastern Indiana, portions of northern Kentucky and\ninto southwest Ohio.  Another potential threat will be damaging \nwinds, however the DCAPE environment is not overly impressive. \n\nThe greatest threat area for flooding will also be the same area as\ntornadoes. While moisture propagation vectors indicate that storms\nwill be moving, there is the potential for multiple rounds of storms\nin the same area.\n\nWill continue to highlight the severe threat and flooding in the HWO\nand other messaging platforms. There will be additional thunderstorm\non Sunday. Widespread showers and some thunderstorms are expected on\nSunday. The greater severe threat will be east of the area, however\ncannot rule out a strong to severe storm. In addition, localized\nheavy rain and flooding will be possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nOff and on thunderstorm chances will continue into next week. There\nis still uncertainty in the upper low. There are varying solutions \nwith some showing the low being more progressive while others show \nthe low meandering near the area and keeping thunderstorm chances \nlonger around the region. There is more confidence and a lean to keep\nprecipitation chances around longer into the week. After this system\ndoes move out of the area there will still be the potential for \nanother system to work into the area later in the week.",
